<div xmlns:perseus="http://www.perseus.org/meta/perseus.rdfs#" xmlns:dctype="http://purl.org/dc/dcmitype/" xmlns:tufts="http://www.tufts.edu/" xmlns:alph="http://alpheios.net/namespaces/tei" class="alpheios-lex-text_container en">
    <div class="alpheios-lex-entry" lemma-id="n25459" body-key=""><b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Koppa</span></b>  (<b><span class="alpheios-lex-la" lang="lat">Coppa</span></b> ), n.
		indecl., = <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κόππα</span>, 
		<div class="alpheios-lex-lex_sense alpheios-lex-lex_sense1"><b>I.</b><i>a letter of the
				original Greek alphabet</i>, in which it stood between <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">π</span> and <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">ρ</span>,
			and corresponded to the Hebr. <i>koph</i> and the Lat.
				<i>q.</i> It was rejected in the literary
			language, <span class="alpheios-lex-greek" lang="grc">κ</span> taking its place, but
			was retained as a numeral, = 90, <i><span class="en">Quint. 1, 4,
				9</span></i>; <i><span class="en">1, 7,
			10</span></i>. </div>
    </div> <div class="alpheios-lex-footnotes en"> </div>
